<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The package sources this tree restores from, and it is a SHORT LIST ON PURPOSE.

  WHY <clear/> COMES FIRST. Without it, this file only ADDS to whatever the machine's user-level and
  machine-level NuGet.config already list, which is a set this repository does not control and cannot
  see. Clearing first makes the source list a property of the checkout rather than of the developer's
  machine: a package either comes from third_party\nuget or the restore fails and says so. There is
  no third outcome where something arrives from a feed nobody here declared.

  WHAT IS VENDORED AND WHY. Exactly one PackageReference exists in this tree,
  Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp in scripting/csharp/Aver.Design/Aver.Design.csproj, and it and its two
  transitive dependencies are checked in beside dxc, imgui and stb. The comments that used to justify
  NOT vendoring it said the .NET SDK ships Roslyn, so the restore resolved from the machine's package
  cache with no network. THAT WAS FALSE. `C:\Program Files\dotnet\sdk\&lt;v&gt;\` carries exactly one
  restorable .nupkg, FSharp.Core, under FSharp/library-packs. Roslyn is in the SDK as the compiler's
  own DLLs under Roslyn/bincore and has never been a package there. 4.13.0 sat in this machine's cache
  because something had fetched it from nuget.org once, and a first build on a clean machine would
  have gone to the network for it.

  FSharp.Core IS NOT LISTED HERE and does not need to be. The SDK injects its library-packs folder
  through the MSBuild property RestoreAdditionalProjectSources, which is not a NuGet source and is
  therefore not what &lt;clear/&gt; clears. That was measured rather than assumed: with this file in
  place, `dotnet restore scripting/fsharp/Aver.Pcg/Aver.Pcg.fsproj` into an empty packages folder
  resolves fsharp.core and nothing else.

  ADDING A DEPENDENCY IS A DELIBERATE ACT. With nuget.org absent, `dotnet add package` fails rather
  than silently acquiring something. Vendor the .nupkg into third_party\nuget and record the licence:
  the permissive-only rule applies to every one of these.
-->
<configuration>
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="aver-vendored" value="third_party\nuget" />
  </packageSources>
</configuration>
